6.6 Cross Plotting 
The system provides a Cross Plotting feature that allows users to inspect dataset values before selecting X-axis and Y-axis parameters.
When users click the eye icon next to the X-axis or Y-axis field, a Data Browser window is opened.
This Data Browser provides a tabular view of the selected dataset file.
Steps:
•	Select Dataset, Tag, and File.
•	Click the eye icon next to:
	X Axis OR
	Y Axis
•	The Data Browser window will open.
The table displays:
	Row numbers
	Column names (column1, column2, etc.)
	Corresponding data values
•	Users can:
	Scroll through the dataset
	Inspect values in each column
	Identify appropriate columns for plotting
•	After reviewing the data:
	Close the Data Browser
	Select the required column from the dropdown
 
Figure 52: 3D Cross Plotting 
 
Figure 53: Cross Plotting with preview Option 
•	Plot name (Optional)
	User can provide a custom plot name.
	If no custom name given, the system automatically generates a meaningful name based on the dataset and axis selection.
 
Figure 54: Plot Setting 
•	Generate Visualization
	After configuration, the user clicks Generate plot.
	The system submits the selected configuration and starts processing file visualization.
 
Figure 55: Visualization Page 
6.7 Axis Configuration (Log)
•	In the visualization settings, users can configure the Log scale for the X-axis and Y-axis to represent data on a logarithmic scale.
•	The Log scale is useful when the dataset values span across multiple orders of magnitude, allowing better visualization of variations in the data.
•	When the Log option is selected in the X Scale and Y Scale dropdown menus, the axis values are displayed in logarithmic format instead of a standard linear progression
•	When the Log option is selected for either the X Scale or Y Scale, the chart is displayed in semi-log format, where one axis uses a logarithmic scale while the other axis remains in standard linear scale. This helps visualize data that spans multiple orders of magnitude along one axis.
•	X-axis and Y-axis required for all plots
•	Z-axis required for:
•	Contour
•	3D plots
Scale Options:
•	Linear
•	Logarithmic
•	Semi-log
 
Figure 56: Log Scale Selection in Axis Configuration
                                                                                                                                                 
Figure 57: Visualization with Logarithmic Axis Scale

 
	Figure 58: Visualization with semi-log Axis Scale
6.8 Large Dataset Handling
To support large datasets:
•	Chunk-based processing
•	Progressive rendering
•	Data preview limits
•	Tile-based loading (LOD)
Benefits:
•	Faster performance
•	Smooth zooming and interaction
6.9 MAT File Visualization
The system provides a dedicated interface for visualizing MATLAB (.mat) files. Unlike standard datasets, .mat files contain variables that must be selected before generating plots.
MAT File Selection
In the Visualization module:
•	Select Dataset
•	Select Tag (MAT_FILE)
•	Select the required .mat file from the File dropdown
•	After selecting the .mat file, the system automatically loads available variables.
Plot Configuration (MAT Plot Setting)
The Plot Setting section allows users to configure visualization for .mat files.
Users can:
•	Select MAT Chart Type (e.g., Scatter)
•	Choose Signature:
•	Plot(X)
•	Plot(X, Y)
Plot Builder
The Plot Builder is used to select variables from the .mat file.
Users must:
•	Select X Variable 
•	Select Y Variable 
Each variable displays:
•	Variable name
•	Dimensions 
Slice Selection
To handle large datasets, slicing is available:
•	Enter slice values 
•	Example: :, 0:10
•	Click Apply (if required)
•	The system will use only the selected portion of data.
•	This improves performance when working with large arrays.
Generating Visualization
•	Click Generate Plot
•	The system processes selected variables and generates the visualization.
•	The generated plot appears in the lower section of the screen.
Users can:
•	View the plotted data
•	Verify the selected variables
•	Save the visualization
 
	Figure 59: MAT File Plot Generation
6.10 Create multiple plot (Over-plot)
	Overplot allows comparison of multiple datasets simultaneously
	Supports:
•	Same file multiple columns
•	Multiple files
•	Cross-dataset comparison
	Ensures:
•	Consistent axis scaling
•	Clear legends
•	Toggle visibility
	Users can add multiple plot series to overlay data on the same chart.
	Each series can:
	Use a different dataset, file, or axis configuration.
	Be individually enabled or disabled
	Removeable if not required
	Users can overlay multiple datasets on the same plot.
	Capabilities:
•	Compare multiple files
•	Compare across datasets (CFD vs Flight)
•	Enable/disable individual series
•	Shared axis configuration
 
Figure 60: Overplot Visualization Page
•	Preview Visualization
	While processing, the system displays status updates.
	Once ready, the generated plot is shown in an interactive preview area.
	Users can zoom, pan, and inspect the plot directly in the preview.
	Users can:
	Zoom
	Pan
	Hover for values
	Toggle series
 
Figure 61: Preview Visualization Page
•	View data tiles (optional)
	For large datasets, the system allows users to preview subsets (tiles) of the underlying data.
	This helps users validate the plots are automatically saved.

•	Save and manage visualizations
	Saved visualization are listed on the same page, where users can:
	Reload a visualization.
	Open in full-screen view
	Delete it if no longer needed
 
Figure 62: Saved visualization files Page
7 Calculation Module
The Calculation module allows users to compute derived parameters from the uploaded datasets. Users select the Dataset, Tag, and File, then enter the required mathematical formula and map the variables to the corresponding dataset columns. After clicking Process Formula, the system calculates the result and displays a preview of the computed values. Once the results are verified, the derived column can be saved for further analysis or visualization.
 
Figure 63: Calculation Page

 
Figure 64: Turn Radius Estimation

s 
Figure 65: True Air Speed (TAS) from Mach
8 Aircraft Performance Module
The Aircraft Performance Module enables users to analyze aircraft performance characteristics using the available dataset. The system allows users to generate different performance plots based on the selected parameters from the input dataset.
Using the available aircraft performance data, users can generate plots to analyze aircraft operational limits, climb performance, aerodynamic characteristics, and maneuvering behavior. These plots help in understanding how the aircraft performs under different flight conditions such as varying speed, altitude, and load factors.
 
Figure 66: Rate of Climb vs Mach Number Plot
 
Figure 67: Aircraft Turn Rate Performance Plot
